// Context-switch throughput gate (#1849) — the Bit counterpart of the Zig test
// `context switch: round trip and microbench sanity (<1us/switch)` in
// `runtime/sched.zig`.
//
// WHY THIS EXISTS. Grep the whole of `tests/` for a throughput assertion —
// microbench, ns/switch, "per switch" — and before this file there were ZERO
// hits. That one Zig unit test was the repo's ONLY performance-regression gate,
// and it lives in a file scheduled for deletion. Meanwhile #1849 records a 4-9x
// slowdown that nothing caught:
//
//     tests/stress/quicwire           BIT_GC=stress   38.3s -> 161s
//     tests/stress/polloneshotdarwin  BIT_GC=stress   13.1s -> 123s
//
// A suite with no throughput assertion cannot tell "still correct" from "still
// correct, four times slower".
//
// WHAT IT MEASURES, AND HOW IT DIFFERS FROM THE ZIG ORIGINAL. The Zig test
// drove `contextSwitch` directly against a hand-built `Context` on a manually
// mapped stack: a raw register-save/restore microbenchmark with no scheduler in
// the picture. That is unreachable from Bit by construction — `Context`,
// `mapGuardedStack` and `contextSwitch` are runtime internals behind the ABI,
// and a Bit program can only reach the scheduler through `spawn` and channels.
// So this measures one level up: an unbuffered-channel ping-pong between two
// green threads, which is the switch PLUS the park/unpark and run-queue work
// that actually surrounds every switch in a real program. That is the more
// useful quantity anyway — #1849's regressions are in that surrounding work,
// not in the register shuffle — but it is a DIFFERENT number, and a strictly
// larger one. See "coverage" at the bottom.
//
// THE HARD PART IS NOT MEASURING, IT IS NOT BEING FLAKY. A wall-clock
// assertion that reddens on a loaded machine is worse than no assertion at all:
// it teaches people to re-run until green, and then a real regression gets
// re-run too. Two mechanisms make this one hold, both chosen from measurements
// on the development host (Apple M-series, 18 cores) and not from taste:
//
//   1. MINIMUM OF `trials`, NOT MEAN. Contention can only ever make a timing
//      worse, never better, so the minimum of N independent trials is the
//      statistic that noise cannot inflate. Measured: under 72 spinning
//      processes on 18 cores (load average 30+), individual trials degraded to
//      4478 ns/switch — 10x — while the minimum over 9 trials stayed at 492
//      against an unloaded 421. The mean would have failed; the minimum did
//      not move.
//
//   2. NORMALIZED RATIOS, NOT ABSOLUTE NANOSECONDS. This was the surprise, and
//      it is why an absolute threshold is not merely portable-unfriendly but
//      wrong even on ONE host: over an hour of measurement, the same binary's
//      minimum went from 421 to 827 ns/switch — 2x — purely from the machine's
//      background state (the calibration loop slowed by the same 1.9x, so the
//      host really was that much slower, not the scheduler). An absolute bound
//      wide enough to survive that (>=2.5x headroom) can no longer see a 4x
//      regression. So the assertion is on ratios against work measured in the
//      SAME process, in the SAME trial loop:
//
//        cpuRatio   round-trip cost / cost of a fixed integer loop.
//                   Cancels host CPU speed, thermal state and contention.
//        chanRatio  round-trip cost / cost of the same number of buffered
//                   channel send+recv pairs on ONE thread, which exercise the
//                   same `chanSend`/`chanRecv` code with no switch in it.
//                   Cancels everything cpuRatio does AND any change in channel
//                   bookkeeping cost, isolating the park/switch/unpark path.
//
//      Both ratios held within ~10% across every condition measured, including
//      the 2x host slowdown and the 4x-oversubscribed load, where the absolute
//      number moved by 2x and 10x respectively.
//
// Two assertions rather than one because each covers the other's blind spot: a
// regression that slows ALL channel traffic (buffered included) leaves
// chanRatio flat and is caught by cpuRatio; a compiler codegen change that
// alters the integer-loop baseline moves cpuRatio and is caught — as a
// contradiction between the two — by chanRatio staying put. Both numbers are
// printed unconditionally on success as well as failure, so a run's output is a
// usable data point rather than just a verdict.
//
// WHAT IT WILL AND WILL NOT CATCH, measured rather than asserted. Inserting
// relay green threads into the ping-pong chain makes each MEASURED switch cost
// a known multiple of real scheduler work — no injected sleeps, no synthetic
// delay, just more park/unpark per round trip — which is a faithful stand-in
// for "the scheduler got N times slower". The sweep on this host:
//
//     relays   true slowdown   cpuRatio   chanRatio   verdict
//        1          1.5x         510        151        pass
//        2          2.0x         701        211        pass
//        3          2.5x         871        262        FAIL
//        6          4.0x        1416        426        FAIL
//     (clean baseline: cpuRatio 348, chanRatio 104)
//
// So the detection floor is ~2.3x and the #1849 class (4-9x) fails by a wide
// margin. Anything milder passes. That is the deliberate price of never crying
// wolf: across ~25 runs spanning an idle host, a host running 2x slower from
// background work, six concurrent copies of this harness, and 72 spinning
// processes on 18 cores, the worst ratios observed were 355 and 113 — so the
// ceilings sit 2.25x and 2.12x above the worst noise, and a 4x regression sits
// 1.8x beyond the ceilings. Tightening the thresholds means first reducing that
// spread, not just lowering the number.
//
// FALSE RED, HOW TO READ ONE. `cpuRatio` high and `chanRatio` normal, with
// `spin` far faster than the recorded baseline, is a codegen improvement to the
// calibration loop, not a scheduler regression — re-baseline `maxCpuRatioX10`.
// Both ratios high is a real scheduler regression. Both normal but the absolute
// `ns/switch` far above the baseline is a slow or busy host, and is exactly the
// case these ratios exist to not fail on.

import { monotonic, sleep } from "std/time"
import { exit } from "std/os"

// --- tunables ---------------------------------------------------------------

// Round trips per timed run. 20k is 17-33ms here depending on host state: long
// enough that timer granularity and one-off scheduling hiccups are noise, short
// enough that all 15 trials of all three measurements cost ~1.2s.
const rounds: int = 20000

// Iterations of the integer calibration loop, sized to cost the same order as
// one ping-pong run so neither dominates the trial.
const spins: int = 2000000

// Independent trials; the minimum of each series is the reported figure. 15
// costs ~1.2s and gives the minimum enough chances that sustained contention
// has to hit EVERY trial to move it.
const trials: int = 15

// Ceilings, as fixed point scaled by 10. Justified above and in the numbers
// below; both were measured with this exact harness on the development host.
//
//   cpuRatioX10   integer-loop iterations of work per context switch.
//                 Observed 316-355 over every condition tried. Ceiling 800.
//   chanRatioX10  buffered send+recv pairs per context switch.
//                 Observed 93-113 over every condition tried. Ceiling 240.
//
// Both ceilings are ~2.2x the worst value ever observed, and both are crossed
// by a 2.5x regression (see the sweep above).
const maxCpuRatioX10: int = 800
const maxChanRatioX10: int = 240

// Converts a deadlock into a failure instead of an infinite stall (docs/development.md:
// "a hang is a failure, not a stall"). This harness spawns no child process, so
// the per-child deadline does not cover it; the guard has to be in-process.
// 120s is ~100x the measured runtime — far above anything a merely slow host
// produces, so firing it means "stuck", never "busy".
const watchdogNs: int = 120000000000

// --- failure ----------------------------------------------------------------

function benchFail(msg: string) {
  eprint("FAIL schedbench: " + msg + "\n")
  exit(1)
}

// Parks on the scheduler's timer queue, so it costs no worker while it waits
// and cannot itself perturb the measurement. If `main` returns first the
// process exits and this never runs.
function benchWatchdog() {
  sleep(watchdogNs)
  eprint("FAIL schedbench: no result after 120s — the ping-pong deadlocked\n")
  exit(1)
}

// --- the measurement --------------------------------------------------------

// Echoes exactly `n` values back, then reports how many it saw. Each `<- a`
// and each `b <- v` on an unbuffered channel is a park + switch, so one loop
// iteration here is one round trip and two context switches.
function benchPong(a: chan<int>, b: chan<int>, done: chan<int>, n: int) {
  let seen: int = 0
  while (seen < n) {
    let v = <- a
    b <- v
    seen = seen + 1
  }
  done <- seen
}

// One timed ping-pong run: `n` round trips over unbuffered channels, returning
// elapsed nanoseconds. Every echoed value is checked against what was sent and
// the partner's own count is checked at the end — a channel that dropped,
// duplicated or short-circuited traffic would otherwise post a spectacular
// throughput number, which is the classic way a benchmark-shaped gate becomes
// vacuous.
function benchRoundTrips(n: int): int {
  let a = chan<int>(0)
  let b = chan<int>(0)
  let done = chan<int>(0)
  spawn benchPong(a, b, done, n)

  let start = monotonic()
  let i: int = 0
  while (i < n) {
    a <- i
    let v = <- b
    if (v != i) {
      benchFail("ping-pong echoed ${v} where ${i} was sent")
    }
    i = i + 1
  }
  let elapsed = monotonic() - start

  let seen = <- done
  if (seen != n) {
    benchFail("partner thread handled ${seen} round trips, expected ${n}")
  }
  return elapsed
}

// Pure integer work, no channel and no scheduler: the host-speed baseline.
// Returns elapsed nanoseconds; the accumulator is returned through a checked
// comparison so the loop cannot be optimized away.
function benchSpinWork(n: int): int {
  let acc: int = 0
  let i: int = 0
  while (i < n) {
    acc = acc + (i ^ (acc >> 3)) + 1
    i = i + 1
  }
  return acc
}

function benchSpin(n: int): int {
  let start = monotonic()
  let acc = benchSpinWork(n)
  let elapsed = monotonic() - start
  if (acc == 0) {
    benchFail("calibration loop produced 0 — it was elided")
  }
  return elapsed
}

// `n` send+recv pairs on a capacity-1 channel from a single thread. The buffer
// is empty at every send and full at every receive, so neither ever blocks:
// this is `chanSend`/`chanRecv` with the park/switch/unpark path removed, which
// is precisely the difference the ping-pong measures.
function benchBuffered(n: int): int {
  let c = chan<int>(1)
  let start = monotonic()
  let i: int = 0
  while (i < n) {
    c <- i
    let v = <- c
    if (v != i) {
      benchFail("buffered channel returned ${v} where ${i} was sent")
    }
    i = i + 1
  }
  return monotonic() - start
}

// --- driver -----------------------------------------------------------------

// Runs the three measurements `trials` times each, interleaved so that any
// slow period on the host lands on all three rather than on one, and returns
// the minimum of each series: (ping-pong, spin, buffered) nanoseconds.
function benchMin(a: int, b: int): int {
  if (a < b) {
    return a
  }
  return b
}

function benchTrials(): (int, int, int) {
  // Seeded above any measurement the watchdog would ever let through (1000s
  // against a 120s deadline), so the first trial always wins and no
  // first-iteration special case is needed. The guard after the loop proves a
  // real measurement replaced it rather than the seed leaking into the result.
  let bestSwitch: int = 1000000000000
  let bestSpin: int = 1000000000000
  let bestBuf: int = 1000000000000

  let t: int = 0
  while (t < trials) {
    let sw = benchRoundTrips(rounds)
    let sp = benchSpin(spins)
    let bf = benchBuffered(rounds)
    if (sw <= 0 || sp <= 0 || bf <= 0) {
      benchFail("monotonic clock did not advance (switch=${sw} spin=${sp} buf=${bf})")
    }
    bestSwitch = benchMin(bestSwitch, sw)
    bestSpin = benchMin(bestSpin, sp)
    bestBuf = benchMin(bestBuf, bf)
    t = t + 1
  }
  if (bestSwitch >= 1000000000000 || bestSpin >= 1000000000000 || bestBuf >= 1000000000000) {
    benchFail("no trial recorded a measurement (trials=${trials})")
  }
  return bestSwitch, bestSpin, bestBuf
}

function main() {
  spawn benchWatchdog()
  let (bestSwitch, bestSpin, bestBuf) = benchTrials()

  // Two switches per round trip, matching the Zig original's convention.
  let switches = rounds * 2
  let nsPerSwitch = bestSwitch / switches
  let cpuRatioX10 = (bestSwitch * spins * 10) / (bestSpin * switches)
  let chanRatioX10 = (bestSwitch * 10) / (bestBuf * 2)

  print("schedbench: ${nsPerSwitch} ns/switch, cpuRatio ${cpuRatioX10}/10 (max ${maxCpuRatioX10}/10), chanRatio ${chanRatioX10}/10 (max ${maxChanRatioX10}/10)\n")
  print("schedbench: best of ${trials} trials — pingpong ${bestSwitch}ns, spin ${bestSpin}ns, buffered ${bestBuf}ns\n")

  if (cpuRatioX10 > maxCpuRatioX10) {
    benchFail("context switch costs ${cpuRatioX10}/10 calibration iterations, ceiling is ${maxCpuRatioX10}/10 (${nsPerSwitch} ns/switch)")
  }
  if (chanRatioX10 > maxChanRatioX10) {
    benchFail("context switch costs ${chanRatioX10}/10 buffered channel ops, ceiling is ${maxChanRatioX10}/10 (${nsPerSwitch} ns/switch)")
  }
  print("OK schedbench\n")
}

// COVERAGE RELATIVE TO THE ZIG ORIGINAL. The original also asserted
// `iterations == round_trips`, that the partner fiber really ran; the
// equivalent here is the `done` count plus the per-value echo check, and it is
// stricter, since the original's counter could not detect reordering. What is
// NOT reproduced is the bare `contextSwitch` path: the original built a
// `Context` by hand and switched into a manually mapped guarded stack with no
// task, no run queue and no channel, so it measured register save/restore
// alone and would catch a regression confined to the assembly. Here that cost
// is a fraction of a round trip that also includes park, unpark, queueing and
// possibly a cross-worker wakeup, so an assembly-only regression is diluted.
// Reaching it needs runtime internals a Bit program cannot address; if the
// bare-switch number is wanted after `runtime/sched.zig` retires, it has to
// come from a Bit runtime benchmark on the runtime side of the ABI, not from
// here.
